ML_PAGE("l3-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 3 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l3\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the principle</b><p>Define the concept accurately in the chapter's terms: descriptive vs analytical information, index, broker and account types, order types.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Show <em>why</em> the relationship exists: the economic cause, not just the result.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply and analyse</b><p>Connect it to the scenario: the investor's objective (speed or price?), the reliability of the source, the right benchmark, the costs.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Weigh alternatives, name a limitation or assumption, and give a justified, conditional conclusion.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Chapter 3 rewards judgment: the best order, broker or source is the one that matches the investor's objective, and every choice has a trade-off.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate the suitability of a stop-loss order for managing investment risk.</p>\n        <p>\"A stop-loss order automatically sells shares when the price drops to a certain level. It helps investors avoid losses.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Overstates the protection: misses that the stop price is not guaranteed.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"A sell stop-loss order initiates a sale when the market price reaches a set trigger. Once activated it becomes a market order, helping the investor exit an unfavourable position, but the stop price does not guarantee the execution price: if bad news drops a share from RM20 to RM14 overnight, a stop at RM18 may execute far below RM18. A stop-limit order gives more control over the minimum price but may not execute at all if prices fall below its limit. The right order depends on whether the investor prioritises exiting the position or avoiding a poor price.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Mechanism + limitation + an alternative + a conditional conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Online investing and investment information (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian university student begins investing through an online trading platform. The platform provides financial news, stock screeners, price quotations, company reports and investment discussion forums.</p>\n<p>The student encounters a social-media recommendation stating:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"TechStar's revenue increased by 18% last year. The stock will double within six months. Institutional investors are secretly buying, so purchase immediately before the price increases.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The student wants to invest RM20,000 based on the recommendation.</p>\n<p>(a) [5 marks] Differentiate descriptive and analytical investment information, using the scenario to illustrate both concepts.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Identify relevant sources of investment information from the chapter and explain how they could help verify the recommendation.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Critically evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of using the Internet as an investment research and trading tool.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Recommend how the student should evaluate the recommendation before committing capital, referring to the chapter's investor-protection principles.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objectives 1 and 2</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Descriptive versus analytical information</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes investment information into two main types.</p>\n<p>Descriptive information refers to factual data concerning the past behavior of the economy, market, industry, company or investment.</p>\n<p>In the scenario, the statement that TechStar's revenue increased by 18% last year is descriptive information, provided that it accurately reflects the company's financial records.</p>\n<p>Analytical information combines current available data with projections, interpretations or recommendations about investment opportunities.</p>\n<p>The prediction that TechStar's share price will double within six months is analytical information because it involves a forecast about future performance.</p>\n<p>The distinction matters because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Historical Facts}\\neq\\text{Future Predictions}}$$</div>\n<p>Historical revenue growth does not guarantee that the company's stock price will increase. Investors must examine the assumptions connecting business performance with expected market value.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the claim that institutional investors are secretly accumulating shares should not be accepted as factual without verifiable supporting evidence.</p>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks for correctly defining the categories, 2 marks for applying them to the scenario and 1 mark for recognising the distinction between evidence and forecasts.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Relevant information sources</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies company websites, shareholder reports, Form 10-K filings, business periodicals, brokerage research reports and investment information services as sources of investment information.</p>\n<p>The student should first examine the company's published financial reports to verify the claimed revenue growth.</p>\n<p>For a U.S. public company, its Form 10-K annual filing would provide formal corporate disclosures that can help assess reported financial performance and business risks.</p>\n<p>The student can also consult business periodicals and brokerage reports to obtain additional analysis concerning the company and its industry.</p>\n<p>However, brokerage recommendations remain analytical opinions and should not automatically be treated as objective facts.</p>\n<p>The chapter also discusses Regulation FD, which requires important corporate information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public in its U.S. regulatory context.</p>\n<p>A reliable analysis should distinguish verified company information from unsupported claims appearing in discussion forums.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Advantages and disadvantages of online investing</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Online investing offers several advantages.</p>\n<p>First, it provides convenient access to financial data, investment education, company reports, stock screening and price quotations.</p>\n<p>Second, it allows investors to research and execute transactions more easily without relying entirely on traditional face-to-face brokerage arrangements.</p>\n<p>Third, online tools can support investment monitoring by allowing investors to track historical prices, portfolio values and market developments.</p>\n<p>However, these benefits introduce several risks.</p>\n<p>Online information may include misleading opinions, exaggerated forecasts or deliberately false statements.</p>\n<p>The chapter warns about stock manipulators, pump-and-dump schemes and get-rich-quick promotions.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the convenience of executing trades can encourage frequent transactions that generate additional commissions and other costs.</p>\n<p>The chapter also warns that online investors may lack a live broker acting as a safety net and must understand the risks of margin trading.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: Online investing improves access to information and transaction services, but greater accessibility does not necessarily produce better investment decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating the recommendation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The student should not immediately commit RM20,000 merely because the recommendation promises substantial returns.</p>\n<p>The chapter recommends asking five investor-protection questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the seller licensed?</li>\n<li>Is the investment registered?</li>\n<li>How do the risks compare with potential rewards?</li>\n<li>Does the investor understand the investment?</li>\n<li>Where can the investor seek assistance if problems arise?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In addition, the student should verify the revenue information against company disclosures and assess whether the claim about institutional buying can be substantiated.</p>\n<p>The investor should also consider the promoter's incentives. An individual who already owns the stock may benefit from encouraging others to purchase it.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The recommendation should be evaluated using credible financial evidence and risk analysis rather than urgency, promotional claims or unsupported forecasts.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>The existence of genuine historical financial data does not validate every prediction built around that data. An investment narrative can combine true facts with unsupported conclusions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Market averages, indexes and investment performance (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor manages a portfolio consisting primarily of large U.S. technology stocks and smaller U.S. growth companies.</p>\n<p>During one year, the investor earns a portfolio return of 12%.</p>\n<p>Over the same period, assume the following hypothetical returns:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benchmark</th>\n<th>Annual return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dow Jones Industrial Average</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasdaq Composite</td>\n<td>19%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 2000</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor concludes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"My portfolio performed well because it earned a positive return and outperformed the Dow Jones Industrial Average.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(a) [5 marks] Explain three purposes of market averages and indexes, and distinguish a market average from an index.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Compare the DJIA, S&amp;P 500, Nasdaq Composite and Russell 2000 in terms of their investment coverage.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Evaluate the investor's performance using the provided data. Explain why benchmark selection affects the conclusion.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Explain how index construction and bond market yield indicators provide additional information for investors.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 3</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Functions of averages and indexes</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies three principal purposes of stock market averages and indexes.</p>\n<p>First, they help investors gauge general market conditions by summarizing the behavior of selected securities.</p>\n<p>Second, they provide benchmarks for evaluating portfolio performance relative to the broader market or a particular investment segment.</p>\n<p>Third, they support the study of historical market trends, cycles and behavior that may inform expectations about future market conditions.</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes averages and indexes by describing market averages as measures of arithmetic price behavior, while indexes measure the value or price behavior of a group of securities relative to an earlier base value.</p>\n<p>Both are useful indicators, but their construction and composition affect what they represent.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing four market indicators</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n<th>Coverage in the chapter</th>\n<th>Principal relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DJIA</td>\n<td>30 major diversified U.S. stocks</td>\n<td>A widely followed measure of selected large companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>500 stocks from major industries</td>\n<td>A broader measure of major U.S. equities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasdaq Composite</td>\n<td>Stocks traded on the Nasdaq market</td>\n<td>Often used to examine technology-oriented market performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 2000</td>\n<td>Approximately 2,000 smaller U.S. companies</td>\n<td>Indicator of small-company equity performance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The S&amp;P 500 provides broader coverage than the 30-stock DJIA in the chapter's comparison.</p>\n<p>The Nasdaq Composite is relevant to portfolios with substantial exposure to Nasdaq-listed companies, including technology companies.</p>\n<p>The Russell 2000 provides a more relevant comparison for portfolios concentrated in smaller U.S. companies.</p>\n<p>The underlying principle is benchmark relevance.</p>\n<p>A benchmark should represent the asset classes, market segment and investment characteristics being evaluated.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluating the investor's performance</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The portfolio returned 12%.</p>\n<p>Relative performance can be calculated as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Relative Performance} = R_{\\text{portfolio}}-R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benchmark</th>\n<th>Benchmark return</th>\n<th>Portfolio relative performance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DJIA</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>+3 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>-3 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasdaq Composite</td>\n<td>19%</td>\n<td>-7 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 2000</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n<td>+5 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor is correct that the portfolio generated a positive return and outperformed the DJIA.</p>\n<p>However, that conclusion is incomplete.</p>\n<p>The portfolio underperformed the S&amp;P 500 and Nasdaq Composite during the same period.</p>\n<p>Since the investor primarily owns technology and smaller growth-company stocks, the DJIA alone may be an unsuitable benchmark.</p>\n<p>The Nasdaq Composite may provide a relevant perspective for the technology exposure, while the Russell 2000 may help evaluate smaller-company holdings.</p>\n<p>However, neither index necessarily represents the portfolio perfectly.</p>\n<p>A more suitable evaluation could use a combination of benchmarks reflecting the portfolio's actual allocation.</p>\n<p>The investor should also evaluate the level of risk accepted to obtain the 12% return.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: A positive absolute return is not necessarily evidence of strong relative performance. The judgment depends on choosing benchmarks that appropriately represent the portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Index methodology and bond yield indicators</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Index construction affects investment performance measurement.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies the Value Line Composite Index as using equal weighting, reducing the extent to which companies with large market values dominate index behavior.</p>\n<p>Therefore, differences in security selection and weighting can produce different index returns, even under similar economic conditions.</p>\n<p>For fixed-income investments, the chapter discusses bond yields as annualized measures of return based on purchasing a bond at its current price and holding it to maturity, incorporating interest and the effect of the difference between purchase price and maturity value.</p>\n<p>These indicators help investors interpret bond market conditions and compare fixed-income opportunities.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investors should understand what an index measures and how it is constructed rather than assuming every market indicator is interchangeable.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Stockbrokers and brokerage accounts (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Three investors have different needs.</p>\n<p>Investor A is experienced, conducts independent research and wants inexpensive trade execution.</p>\n<p>Investor B is relatively inexperienced and needs research, financial guidance and assistance with investment decisions.</p>\n<p>Investor C wants a professional to make portfolio investment decisions on their behalf.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, Investor D wants to purchase securities partly using borrowed money.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Explain the main functions and services of stockbrokers, including the meaning of holding securities in street name.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Compare full-service brokers, premium discount brokers and basic discount brokers. Recommend a suitable type for Investors A and B.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Differentiate cash, margin, custodial and wrap accounts, and explain which arrangements best fit Investors C and D.</p>\n<p>(d) [3 marks] Evaluate two important considerations when selecting a stockbroker, including the problem of churning.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Stockbroker functions and street-name holdings</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies stockbrokers as intermediaries that facilitate securities transactions between buyers and sellers.</p>\n<p>Their primary responsibility is to execute clients' purchase and sale orders at favorable available prices.</p>\n<p>Depending on the brokerage firm's service model, brokers may also provide research reports, investment information and account statements recording transactions.</p>\n<p>The chapter also discusses securities held in street name.</p>\n<p>Under this arrangement, securities are registered in the brokerage firm's name but held for the client who remains the beneficial owner.</p>\n<p>The investor therefore retains the economic ownership interest even though the securities are registered administratively under the brokerage firm.</p>\n<p>This arrangement supports the administration and processing of securities transactions.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing brokerage services</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Brokerage type</th>\n<th>Services in the chapter</th>\n<th>Main trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full-service broker</td>\n<td>Broad services, research and investment advice</td>\n<td>Higher service fees or commissions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium discount broker</td>\n<td>Lower commissions with limited research or advice</td>\n<td>Lower cost but less comprehensive assistance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic discount broker</td>\n<td>Primarily online execution with little or no advice</td>\n<td>Lower execution cost but greater investor responsibility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Investor A</p>\n<p>Investor A conducts independent research and wants inexpensive execution.</p>\n<p>A basic discount broker is likely to be appropriate because Investor A does not require extensive advisory services.</p>\n<p>Paying for unnecessary research or financial guidance may reduce net returns without providing corresponding benefits.</p>\n<p>Investor B</p>\n<p>Investor B is inexperienced and requires guidance.</p>\n<p>A full-service broker may be more appropriate because it offers investment research and advisory assistance.</p>\n<p>However, Investor B should consider whether the additional services justify the higher costs and whether the broker understands their financial goals.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The best brokerage service depends on the investor's expertise and service requirements rather than assuming that the most expensive or cheapest broker is universally superior.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Comparing brokerage accounts</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies four major brokerage account types.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Account</th>\n<th>Main characteristic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash account</td>\n<td>Transactions must be financed using available funds rather than margin borrowing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin account</td>\n<td>The brokerage firm extends borrowing privileges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custodial account</td>\n<td>An adult manages an account for a minor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrap account</td>\n<td>A professional money manager makes investment decisions for an annual fee</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Investor C wishes to delegate investment decisions to a professional manager.</p>\n<p>A wrap account best matches the arrangement described in the chapter because it provides discretionary professional investment management in exchange for an annual fee.</p>\n<p>Investor D wants to finance part of a securities purchase using borrowed funds.</p>\n<p>A margin account is appropriate for that transaction structure, subject to eligibility and applicable requirements.</p>\n<p>However, margin borrowing introduces interest expenses, magnified percentage losses and the possibility of margin calls.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Selecting a stockbroker</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>First, investors should assess whether the broker's experience, investing style and services align with their financial objectives and needs.</p>\n<p>Second, investors should examine the broker's fees and compensation incentives.</p>\n<p>The chapter warns against churning, where a broker encourages excessive trading to increase commissions.</p>\n<p>Churning can harm clients because unnecessary transactions generate costs without necessarily improving investment performance.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Brokerage selection should consider service suitability, competence, compensation and potential conflicts of interest rather than commissions alone.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Trading orders and execution risk (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor holds 1,000 shares of ABC Berhad, currently trading at RM20 each.</p>\n<p>The investor is concerned that unexpected bad news could cause the price to decline significantly.</p>\n<p>At the same time, another investor wants to purchase shares in XYZ Berhad, currently quoted around RM12, but is unwilling to pay more than RM11.</p>\n<p>Consider the following two situations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Situation A: ABC Berhad falls from RM20 to RM14 overnight.</li>\n<li>Situation B: XYZ Berhad continues trading above RM11 for the entire day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Compare market orders and limit orders, and recommend an appropriate order for the investor purchasing XYZ Berhad.</p>\n<p>(b) [8 marks] Differentiate stop-loss and stop-limit orders. Analyse what could happen if the ABC Berhad investor uses a sell stop at RM18, versus a sell stop-limit with a stop of RM18 and a limit of RM17.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Explain fill-or-kill, day and good-'til-canceled orders, and discuss how order duration and execution conditions affect trading decisions.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5 · High priority</em></p>\n<p>This is one of the most important topics for subjective questions because you must distinguish between price control and execution certainty.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Market orders and limit orders</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>A market order instructs a broker to purchase or sell securities at the best price available when the order is executed.</p>\n<p>Its principal advantage is execution speed.</p>\n<p>However, it does not guarantee the final transaction price.</p>\n<p>A limit order specifies an acceptable price boundary.</p>\n<p>A buy limit order instructs the broker to purchase a security at or below a specified price.</p>\n<p>A sell limit order instructs the broker to sell a security at or above a specified price.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Market order</th>\n<th>Limit order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary objective</td>\n<td>Fast execution</td>\n<td>Price control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Execution</td>\n<td>Usually more likely when a market is available</td>\n<td>Not guaranteed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exact price protection</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes, through the limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risk</td>\n<td>Unfavorable execution price</td>\n<td>Order may remain unfilled</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the scenario, XYZ Berhad trades around RM12, but the investor refuses to pay more than RM11.</p>\n<p>The appropriate instruction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Buy Limit Order at RM11}}$$</div>\n<p>This allows execution at RM11 or below, subject to available sellers.</p>\n<p>If the market remains above RM11, the order will not execute.</p>\n<p>This is consistent with the investor's price constraint.</p>\n<p>A market buy order would be unsuitable because it could execute above RM11.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Stop-loss versus stop-limit orders</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>The ABC Berhad investor owns 1,000 shares initially trading at RM20.</p>\n<p>The initial market value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0=1{,}000(20)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}20{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>Alternative 1: Sell stop-loss at RM18</p>\n<p>The chapter describes a stop-loss order as an instruction that becomes a market order when the stop level is triggered.</p>\n<p>The investor establishes a sell stop at RM18.</p>\n<p>However, adverse news causes the market price to fall to RM14 overnight.</p>\n<p>The stop level may be crossed without an opportunity to execute at RM18.</p>\n<p>Once triggered, the stop order becomes a market sell order.</p>\n<p>If execution occurs at RM14:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sale Proceeds}=1{,}000(14)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}14{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The decline from the initial RM20,000 market value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$20{,}000-14{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}6{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>This corresponds to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{-6{,}000}{20{,}000}\\times100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-30\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Had the order executed at RM18, the decline would have been only RM2,000, or 10%.</p>\n<p>But the stop-loss order does not guarantee that outcome.</p>\n<p>The actual execution price depends on market conditions and available counterparties.</p>\n<p>Alternative 2: Sell stop-limit at RM18 stop, RM17 limit</p>\n<p>Now suppose the investor establishes a stop-limit order with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Stop}=\\text{RM}18$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Limit}=\\text{RM}17$$</div>\n<p>When the stop is triggered, the order becomes a sell limit order.</p>\n<p>This means the investor will not sell below RM17 under the stated limit.</p>\n<p>However, if the stock falls directly to RM14 and there are no available purchasers at RM17 or above, the order may remain unfilled.</p>\n<p>The investor retains the shares.</p>\n<p>The market value at RM14 remains RM14,000, representing a RM6,000 decline from the initial value, but no sale has occurred.</p>\n<p>The investor may also experience additional losses if the share price continues falling.</p>\n<p>Comparison</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Consideration</th>\n<th>Stop-loss</th>\n<th>Stop-limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trigger</td>\n<td>RM18</td>\n<td>RM18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Order after triggering</td>\n<td>Market sell</td>\n<td>Limit sell at RM17</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May execute at RM14?</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No, not under the RM17 sell limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guarantees exit?</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risk</td>\n<td>Slippage</td>\n<td>Non-execution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Evaluation: Neither order is universally superior.</p>\n<p>A stop-loss order prioritizes attempting to exit the position after an unfavorable price movement.</p>\n<p>A stop-limit order prioritizes minimum acceptable execution price but may fail to close the position during a sharp market decline.</p>\n<p>The investor should select the order based on whether immediate risk reduction or price protection is more important.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Order timing and execution conditions</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Fill-or-kill order</p>\n<p>The chapter describes a fill-or-kill order as a limit order canceled if it cannot be filled immediately.</p>\n<p>For example, an investor who wishes to purchase 1,000 shares immediately at a specified limit may use a fill-or-kill instruction when partial execution is unacceptable.</p>\n<p>Day order</p>\n<p>A day order remains active during the specified trading day but expires if it is not executed by the end of the day.</p>\n<p>For example, the XYZ investor could place a day buy limit at RM11.</p>\n<p>If the stock never reaches an executable price of RM11 or below, the order expires at the end of the trading day.</p>\n<p>Good-'til-canceled order</p>\n<p>A good-'til-canceled (GTC) order remains active beyond the current trading day until it is executed, canceled or reaches an applicable expiration limit.</p>\n<p>The 2014 slides describe a six-month period; actual validity depends on the brokerage's rules and should not be assumed identical today.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The order's price conditions and duration should reflect the investor's requirements. A correctly chosen order type can protect specific trading constraints, but it cannot eliminate uncertainty about execution.</p>\n<p><strong>Examiner's key distinction:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Stop Price}\\neq\\text{Guaranteed Execution Price}}$$</div>\n<p>A stop-loss order attempts to exit after a trigger. A stop-limit order imposes a price boundary but may leave the investor holding the security.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Transaction costs and investor protection (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor purchases 500 shares at RM10 each and sells them three months later at RM10.60 each.</p>\n<p>The broker charges RM30 commission on purchase and RM30 on sale. Assume there are no additional fees or taxes.</p>\n<p>After the transaction, the investor learns that their broker had recommended frequent trading primarily to generate commissions.</p>\n<p>Another client of the brokerage suffers a loss because the broker becomes financially insolvent.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the gross trading profit, net trading profit and net holding-period return based on the investor's total initial cash outflow.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Explain how transaction costs and frequent trading can influence investment performance.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Explain the role and limitations of the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) as described in the chapter, distinguishing broker failure from investment losses.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Compare mediation and arbitration as mechanisms for resolving disputes between investors and brokers.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Trading profit and net return</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investor purchases:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$500\\text{ shares}\\times \\text{RM}10$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Purchase Cost}=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The purchase commission is RM30.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the total initial cash outflow is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000+30$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}5{,}030}$$</div>\n<p>The investor later sells the shares at RM10.60.</p>\n<p>Gross sale proceeds:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$500(10.60)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}5{,}300$$</div>\n<p>The gross trading profit before commissions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}300-5{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}300}$$</div>\n<p>Total commissions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$30+30=\\text{RM}60$$</div>\n<p>Net trading profit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$300-60$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}240}$$</div>\n<p>Alternatively:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net Profit} = (5{,}300-30)-(5{,}000+30)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=5{,}270-5{,}030$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}240$$</div>\n<p>Since the question asks for the return on the total initial cash outflow:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{240}{5{,}030}\\times100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{net}}\\approx4.77\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Gross profit</th>\n<th>Total commissions</th>\n<th>Net profit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM300</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td><strong>RM240</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor earns a gross price return of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{300}{5{,}000}\\times100=6\\%$$</div>\n<p>but the actual return after the stated transaction costs is lower.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates why investment performance should be evaluated after relevant costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Transaction costs and frequent trading</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter discusses commissions as an important cost of securities transactions.</p>\n<p>Frequent trading increases the number of transactions and therefore may increase total commissions.</p>\n<p>For example, assuming the same RM30 commission applies each time, five round-trip trades would incur:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5(30+30)=\\text{RM}300$$</div>\n<p>in commissions alone.</p>\n<p>If gross trading gains are small, transaction costs can eliminate much of the expected profit.</p>\n<p>The chapter also identifies day trading as risky, particularly when margin is involved, and notes that frequent trading can generate substantial brokerage commissions.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: The investor should not evaluate a trading strategy only by its gross gains. The relevant outcome is the return after trading costs and other applicable expenses.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) SIPC protection and limitations</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) as providing protection against certain financial failures of brokerage firms.</p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish the failure of a brokerage intermediary from losses arising from the investment itself.</p>\n<p>For example, a broker may become insolvent while holding customers' securities or cash.</p>\n<p>SIPC protection concerns certain customer claims associated with such broker failures.</p>\n<p>The 2014 slides state historical protection limits of <span class=\"cur\">$</span>500,000 for securities and <span class=\"cur\">$</span>250,000 for cash. These are the figures printed in the chapter, not a verified statement of current coverage.</p>\n<p>However, the chapter explicitly notes that SIPC does not guarantee investors against churning or poor brokerage advice.</p>\n<p>SIPC protection is also not insurance against ordinary market losses.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An investor whose properly held shares fall in value because of poor company earnings has suffered an investment loss.</li>\n<li>An investor whose broker fails and cannot return protected customer property faces a different kind of loss associated with intermediary failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These situations should not be treated as economically equivalent.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investor protection mechanisms can address certain institutional risks, but they do not remove the possibility of investment losses or guarantee the quality of financial advice.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Mediation versus arbitration</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Mediation</th>\n<th>Arbitration</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nature</td>\n<td>Informal, voluntary dispute-resolution process</td>\n<td>More formal procedure before a panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Method</td>\n<td>Parties attempt to reach an agreement</td>\n<td>Parties present arguments to decision-makers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outcome</td>\n<td>Nonbinding if no agreement is reached</td>\n<td>Can result in a binding decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principal function</td>\n<td>Facilitate a negotiated settlement</td>\n<td>Obtain a formal resolution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Under the chapter's description, binding arbitration requires acceptance of the panel's decision and may involve giving up the right to sue over the dispute.</p>\n<p>Mediation may be preferable when both parties are willing to negotiate a resolution.</p>\n<p>Arbitration may be appropriate when a formal determination is required under an applicable agreement.</p>\n<p>The suitability of either mechanism depends on the nature of the dispute and applicable contractual and regulatory conditions.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investor protection involves different mechanisms for different problems. Brokerage failure, unsuitable advice, excessive trading and ordinary investment losses should not be treated as one undifferentiated category.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Investment advisors and investment clubs (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A group of five investors collectively controls RM200,000 in investment capital. They are considering three approaches.</p>\n<p>They could manage the money individually through online discount brokers, appoint an investment advisor to make recommendations, or establish an investment club to pool their financial knowledge and capital.</p>\n<p>An advisor offers services for an annual fee of 1.5% of assets under management. Assume this fee is calculated on the beginning-of-year RM200,000 balance.</p>\n<p>The group expects a gross annual investment return of 8% before advisory fees.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Explain the role of an investment advisor and the factors investors should evaluate before selecting one.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate the annual advisory fee and the net return after that fee under the stated assumptions. Evaluate the financial significance.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Explain the structure and purpose of investment clubs, including their potential advantages and limitations.</p>\n<p>(d) [5 marks] Recommend a suitable investment decision-making approach for the group, considering expertise, cost, responsibility and investment objectives.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Investment advisors and selection criteria</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>An investment advisor provides professional assistance in making investment decisions.</p>\n<p>Depending on the arrangement, an advisor may help clients understand investment choices, analyse securities, develop strategies and manage portfolios.</p>\n<p>The chapter discusses several considerations when choosing an investment advisor.</p>\n<p>First, investors should consider the advisor's experience and track record.</p>\n<p>Second, they should evaluate the advisor's professional qualifications.</p>\n<p>The slides list qualifications such as CFA, CIMA, CFP, CIC, ChFC, CLU and CPA.</p>\n<p>Third, investors should consider the advisor's reputation, fees and ability to understand the client's objectives.</p>\n<p>The chapter emphasizes that a good advisor should ask investors numerous questions to understand their financial expertise and circumstances.</p>\n<p>It also states that regulatory registration does not guarantee competence.</p>\n<p>This means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Registration}\\neq\\text{Guaranteed Competence}}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Professional Advice}\\neq\\text{Guaranteed Returns}}$$</div>\n<p>An investor should therefore evaluate whether the advisor's services offer sufficient benefits relative to their costs and limitations.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Annual advisory fee and net return</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The group has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets Under Management}=\\text{RM}200{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The annual advisory fee is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1.5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Assuming fees are based on beginning-of-year assets:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Annual Fee}=200{,}000(0.015)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}3{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The expected gross investment return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Expected gross earnings:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$200{,}000(0.08)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}16{,}000$$</div>\n<p>After deducting the advisory fee:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net Earnings}=16{,}000-3{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}13{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{13{,}000}{200{,}000}\\times100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{6.5\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Annual return</th>\n<th>Earnings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gross</td>\n<td>8.0%</td>\n<td>RM16,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After advisory fee</td>\n<td><strong>6.5%</strong></td>\n<td>RM13,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Evaluation: The advisor's annual fee reduces the expected return by 1.5 percentage points under the question's assumptions.</p>\n<p>This does not automatically mean that professional advice is poor value.</p>\n<p>The advisor may provide financial planning, risk management, investment research or better decision-making discipline.</p>\n<p>However, those benefits should justify the costs.</p>\n<p>The group should not assume that hiring an advisor guarantees returns exceeding those available through a lower-cost approach.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investment clubs</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines an investment club as a legal partnership established by investors to pool capital and knowledge.</p>\n<p>Members jointly research investment opportunities, recommend securities and analyse investment performance.</p>\n<p>Potential advantages include shared investment knowledge and learning opportunities.</p>\n<p>Pooling financial resources may also allow investors to access opportunities that would be difficult to pursue individually.</p>\n<p>Members can contribute different skills and perspectives to investment analysis.</p>\n<p>However, collective decision-making does not automatically guarantee better decisions.</p>\n<p>Members may disagree about suitable investments, accept excessive risk because of group enthusiasm or fail to examine recommendations critically.</p>\n<p>Therefore, an investment club requires a disciplined decision-making process and clear agreement on investment objectives and responsibilities.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Recommendation</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The group should choose its arrangement based on members' expertise, willingness to manage investments, required services and acceptable costs.</p>\n<p>If the members possess adequate financial knowledge and can independently research securities, discount brokerage services may provide a lower-cost execution mechanism.</p>\n<p>If the group needs substantial professional assistance, employing an investment advisor may be appropriate, provided the expected benefits justify the fees.</p>\n<p>If the members want to combine their capital while developing investment knowledge collaboratively, an investment club may be suitable.</p>\n<p>However, the group should establish clear investment objectives, roles, risk limits and rules for making decisions before pooling funds.</p>\n<p>A particularly sensible approach may involve combining shared investment research with professional advice where specialized expertise is needed.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The most appropriate arrangement is not necessarily the lowest-cost option or the one employing the most professionals. It is the arrangement that provides a suitable balance of expertise, responsibility, services, costs and investment objectives.</p></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"l3s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">7 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. How to construct a high-scoring answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s1\" title=\"1. How to construct a high-scoring answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>How to construct a high-scoring answer</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s2\" title=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Full mock subjective examination</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s3\" title=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s4\" title=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Additional short-answer questions for revision</button></li><li data-full=\"5. How to allocate your marks and time\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s5\" title=\"5. How to allocate your marks and time\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>How to allocate your marks and time</button></li><li data-full=\"6. What I would prioritize for Chapter 3\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s6\" title=\"6. What I would prioritize for Chapter 3\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>What I would prioritize for Chapter 3</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Final high-level examination exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3s-s7\" title=\"7. Final high-level examination exercise\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Final high-level examination exercise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 3 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>7 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 3 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Investment Information and Securities Transactions</p>\n<p>Chapter 3 is especially important for subjective examinations because it tests whether you can transform investment information into a well-reasoned decision and execute that decision appropriately.</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 taught us how securities markets operate. Chapter 3 introduces another layer of responsibility: evaluating information sources, selecting appropriate market benchmarks, choosing brokerage services, placing trading orders, understanding transaction costs, and obtaining advice or investor protection.</p>\n<p>A basic question might ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Differentiate a market order from a limit order.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A higher-level subjective question might ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>An investor wants to purchase shares in a thinly traded company but refuses to pay more than RM12 per share. Recommend an appropriate order, evaluate the execution risks, and explain why an alternative order may be unsuitable.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The second question requires conceptual knowledge, application, analysis and evaluation.</p>\n<p>This preparation follows the six learning goals in your uploaded Chapter 3 slides. The questions and cases are original practice material, while the terminology and subject coverage follow the textbook.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. How to construct a high-scoring answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For Chapter 3, I recommend this approach:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Establish the financial concept:</strong> Define the relevant investment information, benchmark, brokerage arrangement or order type.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Explain the economic mechanism:</strong> Demonstrate how the concept works and why it produces particular advantages or risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Apply the principle to the scenario:</strong> Identify the investor's objective, constraints, information quality and likely consequences.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Evaluate and justify:</strong> Compare alternatives, explain the trade-offs and recommend the most appropriate decision.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Example: Weak answer versus strong answer</h4>\n<p>Question: Evaluate the suitability of a stop-loss order for managing investment risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Weak answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A stop-loss order automatically sells shares when the price drops to a certain level. It helps investors avoid losses.</p>\n<p><strong>Stronger answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A sell stop-loss order is an instruction to initiate the sale of a security when its market price reaches a specified trigger level. Under the chapter's description, the order becomes a market order once activated, helping investors attempt to exit an unfavorable position. However, the stop price does not guarantee the actual execution price. For example, if negative corporate news causes a share price to fall from RM20 to RM14 overnight, a stop order set at RM18 may execute substantially below RM18. A stop-limit order can provide greater control over the minimum sale price but may remain unexecuted if market prices fall below its limit. Therefore, the appropriate order depends on whether the investor prioritizes exiting the position or avoiding an unfavorable execution price.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer explains the mechanism, recognises the limitation, compares an alternative and gives a conditional conclusion.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Full mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 6 · <strong>Total marks:</strong> 120 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 120 min</p>\n<p>Six questions worth 20 marks each. The mock paper is designed to cover all six Chapter 3 learning objectives, with model answers and suggested marking guidance provided below.</p>\n<h4>Question 1 - Online investing and investment information (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A Malaysian university student begins investing through an online trading platform. The platform provides financial news, stock screeners, price quotations, company reports and investment discussion forums.</p>\n<p>The student encounters a social-media recommendation stating:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"TechStar's revenue increased by 18% last year. The stock will double within six months. Institutional investors are secretly buying, so purchase immediately before the price increases.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The student wants to invest RM20,000 based on the recommendation.</p>\n<p>(a) [5 marks] Differentiate descriptive and analytical investment information, using the scenario to illustrate both concepts.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Identify relevant sources of investment information from the chapter and explain how they could help verify the recommendation.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Critically evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of using the Internet as an investment research and trading tool.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Recommend how the student should evaluate the recommendation before committing capital, referring to the chapter's investor-protection principles.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Market averages, indexes and investment performance (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor manages a portfolio consisting primarily of large U.S. technology stocks and smaller U.S. growth companies.</p>\n<p>During one year, the investor earns a portfolio return of 12%.</p>\n<p>Over the same period, assume the following hypothetical returns:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benchmark</th>\n<th>Annual return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dow Jones Industrial Average</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasdaq Composite</td>\n<td>19%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 2000</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor concludes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"My portfolio performed well because it earned a positive return and outperformed the Dow Jones Industrial Average.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(a) [5 marks] Explain three purposes of market averages and indexes, and distinguish a market average from an index.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Compare the DJIA, S&amp;P 500, Nasdaq Composite and Russell 2000 in terms of their investment coverage.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Evaluate the investor's performance using the provided data. Explain why benchmark selection affects the conclusion.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Explain how index construction and bond market yield indicators provide additional information for investors.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Stockbrokers and brokerage accounts (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Three investors have different needs.</p>\n<p>Investor A is experienced, conducts independent research and wants inexpensive trade execution.</p>\n<p>Investor B is relatively inexperienced and needs research, financial guidance and assistance with investment decisions.</p>\n<p>Investor C wants a professional to make portfolio investment decisions on their behalf.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, Investor D wants to purchase securities partly using borrowed money.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Explain the main functions and services of stockbrokers, including the meaning of holding securities in street name.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Compare full-service brokers, premium discount brokers and basic discount brokers. Recommend a suitable type for Investors A and B.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Differentiate cash, margin, custodial and wrap accounts, and explain which arrangements best fit Investors C and D.</p>\n<p>(d) [3 marks] Evaluate two important considerations when selecting a stockbroker, including the problem of churning.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Trading orders and execution risk (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor holds 1,000 shares of ABC Berhad, currently trading at RM20 each.</p>\n<p>The investor is concerned that unexpected bad news could cause the price to decline significantly.</p>\n<p>At the same time, another investor wants to purchase shares in XYZ Berhad, currently quoted around RM12, but is unwilling to pay more than RM11.</p>\n<p>Consider the following two situations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Situation A: ABC Berhad falls from RM20 to RM14 overnight.</li>\n<li>Situation B: XYZ Berhad continues trading above RM11 for the entire day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Compare market orders and limit orders, and recommend an appropriate order for the investor purchasing XYZ Berhad.</p>\n<p>(b) [8 marks] Differentiate stop-loss and stop-limit orders. Analyse what could happen if the ABC Berhad investor uses a sell stop at RM18, versus a sell stop-limit with a stop of RM18 and a limit of RM17.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Explain fill-or-kill, day and good-'til-canceled orders, and discuss how order duration and execution conditions affect trading decisions.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Transaction costs and investor protection (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor purchases 500 shares at RM10 each and sells them three months later at RM10.60 each.</p>\n<p>The broker charges RM30 commission on purchase and RM30 on sale. Assume there are no additional fees or taxes.</p>\n<p>After the transaction, the investor learns that their broker had recommended frequent trading primarily to generate commissions.</p>\n<p>Another client of the brokerage suffers a loss because the broker becomes financially insolvent.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the gross trading profit, net trading profit and net holding-period return based on the investor's total initial cash outflow.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Explain how transaction costs and frequent trading can influence investment performance.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Explain the role and limitations of the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) as described in the chapter, distinguishing broker failure from investment losses.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Compare mediation and arbitration as mechanisms for resolving disputes between investors and brokers.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Investment advisors and investment clubs (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A group of five investors collectively controls RM200,000 in investment capital. They are considering three approaches.</p>\n<p>They could manage the money individually through online discount brokers, appoint an investment advisor to make recommendations, or establish an investment club to pool their financial knowledge and capital.</p>\n<p>An advisor offers services for an annual fee of 1.5% of assets under management. Assume this fee is calculated on the beginning-of-year RM200,000 balance.</p>\n<p>The group expects a gross annual investment return of 8% before advisory fees.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Explain the role of an investment advisor and the factors investors should evaluate before selecting one.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate the annual advisory fee and the net return after that fee under the stated assumptions. Evaluate the financial significance.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Explain the structure and purpose of investment clubs, including their potential advantages and limitations.</p>\n<p>(d) [5 marks] Recommend a suitable investment decision-making approach for the group, considering expertise, cost, responsibility and investment objectives.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Model answers and marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These model answers are designed to demonstrate the reasoning needed for technical subjective questions. The suggested mark allocations are illustrative, not your lecturer's official marking scheme.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 1 - Online investing and investment information</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objectives 1 and 2</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Descriptive versus analytical information</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes investment information into two main types.</p>\n<p>Descriptive information refers to factual data concerning the past behavior of the economy, market, industry, company or investment.</p>\n<p>In the scenario, the statement that TechStar's revenue increased by 18% last year is descriptive information, provided that it accurately reflects the company's financial records.</p>\n<p>Analytical information combines current available data with projections, interpretations or recommendations about investment opportunities.</p>\n<p>The prediction that TechStar's share price will double within six months is analytical information because it involves a forecast about future performance.</p>\n<p>The distinction matters because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Historical Facts}\\neq\\text{Future Predictions}}$$</div>\n<p>Historical revenue growth does not guarantee that the company's stock price will increase. Investors must examine the assumptions connecting business performance with expected market value.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the claim that institutional investors are secretly accumulating shares should not be accepted as factual without verifiable supporting evidence.</p>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks for correctly defining the categories, 2 marks for applying them to the scenario and 1 mark for recognising the distinction between evidence and forecasts.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Relevant information sources</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies company websites, shareholder reports, Form 10-K filings, business periodicals, brokerage research reports and investment information services as sources of investment information.</p>\n<p>The student should first examine the company's published financial reports to verify the claimed revenue growth.</p>\n<p>For a U.S. public company, its Form 10-K annual filing would provide formal corporate disclosures that can help assess reported financial performance and business risks.</p>\n<p>The student can also consult business periodicals and brokerage reports to obtain additional analysis concerning the company and its industry.</p>\n<p>However, brokerage recommendations remain analytical opinions and should not automatically be treated as objective facts.</p>\n<p>The chapter also discusses Regulation FD, which requires important corporate information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public in its U.S. regulatory context.</p>\n<p>A reliable analysis should distinguish verified company information from unsupported claims appearing in discussion forums.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Advantages and disadvantages of online investing</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Online investing offers several advantages.</p>\n<p>First, it provides convenient access to financial data, investment education, company reports, stock screening and price quotations.</p>\n<p>Second, it allows investors to research and execute transactions more easily without relying entirely on traditional face-to-face brokerage arrangements.</p>\n<p>Third, online tools can support investment monitoring by allowing investors to track historical prices, portfolio values and market developments.</p>\n<p>However, these benefits introduce several risks.</p>\n<p>Online information may include misleading opinions, exaggerated forecasts or deliberately false statements.</p>\n<p>The chapter warns about stock manipulators, pump-and-dump schemes and get-rich-quick promotions.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the convenience of executing trades can encourage frequent transactions that generate additional commissions and other costs.</p>\n<p>The chapter also warns that online investors may lack a live broker acting as a safety net and must understand the risks of margin trading.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: Online investing improves access to information and transaction services, but greater accessibility does not necessarily produce better investment decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating the recommendation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The student should not immediately commit RM20,000 merely because the recommendation promises substantial returns.</p>\n<p>The chapter recommends asking five investor-protection questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the seller licensed?</li>\n<li>Is the investment registered?</li>\n<li>How do the risks compare with potential rewards?</li>\n<li>Does the investor understand the investment?</li>\n<li>Where can the investor seek assistance if problems arise?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In addition, the student should verify the revenue information against company disclosures and assess whether the claim about institutional buying can be substantiated.</p>\n<p>The investor should also consider the promoter's incentives. An individual who already owns the stock may benefit from encouraging others to purchase it.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The recommendation should be evaluated using credible financial evidence and risk analysis rather than urgency, promotional claims or unsupported forecasts.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>The existence of genuine historical financial data does not validate every prediction built around that data. An investment narrative can combine true facts with unsupported conclusions.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 2 - Market averages and indexes</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 3</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Functions of averages and indexes</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies three principal purposes of stock market averages and indexes.</p>\n<p>First, they help investors gauge general market conditions by summarizing the behavior of selected securities.</p>\n<p>Second, they provide benchmarks for evaluating portfolio performance relative to the broader market or a particular investment segment.</p>\n<p>Third, they support the study of historical market trends, cycles and behavior that may inform expectations about future market conditions.</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes averages and indexes by describing market averages as measures of arithmetic price behavior, while indexes measure the value or price behavior of a group of securities relative to an earlier base value.</p>\n<p>Both are useful indicators, but their construction and composition affect what they represent.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing four market indicators</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n<th>Coverage in the chapter</th>\n<th>Principal relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DJIA</td>\n<td>30 major diversified U.S. stocks</td>\n<td>A widely followed measure of selected large companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>500 stocks from major industries</td>\n<td>A broader measure of major U.S. equities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasdaq Composite</td>\n<td>Stocks traded on the Nasdaq market</td>\n<td>Often used to examine technology-oriented market performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 2000</td>\n<td>Approximately 2,000 smaller U.S. companies</td>\n<td>Indicator of small-company equity performance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The S&amp;P 500 provides broader coverage than the 30-stock DJIA in the chapter's comparison.</p>\n<p>The Nasdaq Composite is relevant to portfolios with substantial exposure to Nasdaq-listed companies, including technology companies.</p>\n<p>The Russell 2000 provides a more relevant comparison for portfolios concentrated in smaller U.S. companies.</p>\n<p>The underlying principle is benchmark relevance.</p>\n<p>A benchmark should represent the asset classes, market segment and investment characteristics being evaluated.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluating the investor's performance</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The portfolio returned 12%.</p>\n<p>Relative performance can be calculated as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Relative Performance} = R_{\\text{portfolio}}-R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benchmark</th>\n<th>Benchmark return</th>\n<th>Portfolio relative performance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DJIA</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>+3 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>-3 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasdaq Composite</td>\n<td>19%</td>\n<td>-7 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 2000</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n<td>+5 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor is correct that the portfolio generated a positive return and outperformed the DJIA.</p>\n<p>However, that conclusion is incomplete.</p>\n<p>The portfolio underperformed the S&amp;P 500 and Nasdaq Composite during the same period.</p>\n<p>Since the investor primarily owns technology and smaller growth-company stocks, the DJIA alone may be an unsuitable benchmark.</p>\n<p>The Nasdaq Composite may provide a relevant perspective for the technology exposure, while the Russell 2000 may help evaluate smaller-company holdings.</p>\n<p>However, neither index necessarily represents the portfolio perfectly.</p>\n<p>A more suitable evaluation could use a combination of benchmarks reflecting the portfolio's actual allocation.</p>\n<p>The investor should also evaluate the level of risk accepted to obtain the 12% return.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: A positive absolute return is not necessarily evidence of strong relative performance. The judgment depends on choosing benchmarks that appropriately represent the portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Index methodology and bond yield indicators</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Index construction affects investment performance measurement.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies the Value Line Composite Index as using equal weighting, reducing the extent to which companies with large market values dominate index behavior.</p>\n<p>Therefore, differences in security selection and weighting can produce different index returns, even under similar economic conditions.</p>\n<p>For fixed-income investments, the chapter discusses bond yields as annualized measures of return based on purchasing a bond at its current price and holding it to maturity, incorporating interest and the effect of the difference between purchase price and maturity value.</p>\n<p>These indicators help investors interpret bond market conditions and compare fixed-income opportunities.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investors should understand what an index measures and how it is constructed rather than assuming every market indicator is interchangeable.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 3 - Stockbrokers and brokerage accounts</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Stockbroker functions and street-name holdings</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies stockbrokers as intermediaries that facilitate securities transactions between buyers and sellers.</p>\n<p>Their primary responsibility is to execute clients' purchase and sale orders at favorable available prices.</p>\n<p>Depending on the brokerage firm's service model, brokers may also provide research reports, investment information and account statements recording transactions.</p>\n<p>The chapter also discusses securities held in street name.</p>\n<p>Under this arrangement, securities are registered in the brokerage firm's name but held for the client who remains the beneficial owner.</p>\n<p>The investor therefore retains the economic ownership interest even though the securities are registered administratively under the brokerage firm.</p>\n<p>This arrangement supports the administration and processing of securities transactions.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing brokerage services</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Brokerage type</th>\n<th>Services in the chapter</th>\n<th>Main trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full-service broker</td>\n<td>Broad services, research and investment advice</td>\n<td>Higher service fees or commissions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium discount broker</td>\n<td>Lower commissions with limited research or advice</td>\n<td>Lower cost but less comprehensive assistance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic discount broker</td>\n<td>Primarily online execution with little or no advice</td>\n<td>Lower execution cost but greater investor responsibility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Investor A</p>\n<p>Investor A conducts independent research and wants inexpensive execution.</p>\n<p>A basic discount broker is likely to be appropriate because Investor A does not require extensive advisory services.</p>\n<p>Paying for unnecessary research or financial guidance may reduce net returns without providing corresponding benefits.</p>\n<p>Investor B</p>\n<p>Investor B is inexperienced and requires guidance.</p>\n<p>A full-service broker may be more appropriate because it offers investment research and advisory assistance.</p>\n<p>However, Investor B should consider whether the additional services justify the higher costs and whether the broker understands their financial goals.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The best brokerage service depends on the investor's expertise and service requirements rather than assuming that the most expensive or cheapest broker is universally superior.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Comparing brokerage accounts</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies four major brokerage account types.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Account</th>\n<th>Main characteristic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash account</td>\n<td>Transactions must be financed using available funds rather than margin borrowing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin account</td>\n<td>The brokerage firm extends borrowing privileges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custodial account</td>\n<td>An adult manages an account for a minor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrap account</td>\n<td>A professional money manager makes investment decisions for an annual fee</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Investor C wishes to delegate investment decisions to a professional manager.</p>\n<p>A wrap account best matches the arrangement described in the chapter because it provides discretionary professional investment management in exchange for an annual fee.</p>\n<p>Investor D wants to finance part of a securities purchase using borrowed funds.</p>\n<p>A margin account is appropriate for that transaction structure, subject to eligibility and applicable requirements.</p>\n<p>However, margin borrowing introduces interest expenses, magnified percentage losses and the possibility of margin calls.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Selecting a stockbroker</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>First, investors should assess whether the broker's experience, investing style and services align with their financial objectives and needs.</p>\n<p>Second, investors should examine the broker's fees and compensation incentives.</p>\n<p>The chapter warns against churning, where a broker encourages excessive trading to increase commissions.</p>\n<p>Churning can harm clients because unnecessary transactions generate costs without necessarily improving investment performance.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Brokerage selection should consider service suitability, competence, compensation and potential conflicts of interest rather than commissions alone.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 4 - Trading orders and execution risk</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5 · High priority</em></p>\n<p>This is one of the most important topics for subjective questions because you must distinguish between price control and execution certainty.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Market orders and limit orders</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>A market order instructs a broker to purchase or sell securities at the best price available when the order is executed.</p>\n<p>Its principal advantage is execution speed.</p>\n<p>However, it does not guarantee the final transaction price.</p>\n<p>A limit order specifies an acceptable price boundary.</p>\n<p>A buy limit order instructs the broker to purchase a security at or below a specified price.</p>\n<p>A sell limit order instructs the broker to sell a security at or above a specified price.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Market order</th>\n<th>Limit order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary objective</td>\n<td>Fast execution</td>\n<td>Price control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Execution</td>\n<td>Usually more likely when a market is available</td>\n<td>Not guaranteed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exact price protection</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes, through the limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risk</td>\n<td>Unfavorable execution price</td>\n<td>Order may remain unfilled</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the scenario, XYZ Berhad trades around RM12, but the investor refuses to pay more than RM11.</p>\n<p>The appropriate instruction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Buy Limit Order at RM11}}$$</div>\n<p>This allows execution at RM11 or below, subject to available sellers.</p>\n<p>If the market remains above RM11, the order will not execute.</p>\n<p>This is consistent with the investor's price constraint.</p>\n<p>A market buy order would be unsuitable because it could execute above RM11.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Stop-loss versus stop-limit orders</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>The ABC Berhad investor owns 1,000 shares initially trading at RM20.</p>\n<p>The initial market value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0=1{,}000(20)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}20{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>Alternative 1: Sell stop-loss at RM18</p>\n<p>The chapter describes a stop-loss order as an instruction that becomes a market order when the stop level is triggered.</p>\n<p>The investor establishes a sell stop at RM18.</p>\n<p>However, adverse news causes the market price to fall to RM14 overnight.</p>\n<p>The stop level may be crossed without an opportunity to execute at RM18.</p>\n<p>Once triggered, the stop order becomes a market sell order.</p>\n<p>If execution occurs at RM14:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sale Proceeds}=1{,}000(14)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}14{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The decline from the initial RM20,000 market value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$20{,}000-14{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}6{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>This corresponds to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{-6{,}000}{20{,}000}\\times100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-30\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Had the order executed at RM18, the decline would have been only RM2,000, or 10%.</p>\n<p>But the stop-loss order does not guarantee that outcome.</p>\n<p>The actual execution price depends on market conditions and available counterparties.</p>\n<p>Alternative 2: Sell stop-limit at RM18 stop, RM17 limit</p>\n<p>Now suppose the investor establishes a stop-limit order with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Stop}=\\text{RM}18$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Limit}=\\text{RM}17$$</div>\n<p>When the stop is triggered, the order becomes a sell limit order.</p>\n<p>This means the investor will not sell below RM17 under the stated limit.</p>\n<p>However, if the stock falls directly to RM14 and there are no available purchasers at RM17 or above, the order may remain unfilled.</p>\n<p>The investor retains the shares.</p>\n<p>The market value at RM14 remains RM14,000, representing a RM6,000 decline from the initial value, but no sale has occurred.</p>\n<p>The investor may also experience additional losses if the share price continues falling.</p>\n<p>Comparison</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Consideration</th>\n<th>Stop-loss</th>\n<th>Stop-limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trigger</td>\n<td>RM18</td>\n<td>RM18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Order after triggering</td>\n<td>Market sell</td>\n<td>Limit sell at RM17</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May execute at RM14?</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No, not under the RM17 sell limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guarantees exit?</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risk</td>\n<td>Slippage</td>\n<td>Non-execution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Evaluation: Neither order is universally superior.</p>\n<p>A stop-loss order prioritizes attempting to exit the position after an unfavorable price movement.</p>\n<p>A stop-limit order prioritizes minimum acceptable execution price but may fail to close the position during a sharp market decline.</p>\n<p>The investor should select the order based on whether immediate risk reduction or price protection is more important.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Order timing and execution conditions</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Fill-or-kill order</p>\n<p>The chapter describes a fill-or-kill order as a limit order canceled if it cannot be filled immediately.</p>\n<p>For example, an investor who wishes to purchase 1,000 shares immediately at a specified limit may use a fill-or-kill instruction when partial execution is unacceptable.</p>\n<p>Day order</p>\n<p>A day order remains active during the specified trading day but expires if it is not executed by the end of the day.</p>\n<p>For example, the XYZ investor could place a day buy limit at RM11.</p>\n<p>If the stock never reaches an executable price of RM11 or below, the order expires at the end of the trading day.</p>\n<p>Good-'til-canceled order</p>\n<p>A good-'til-canceled (GTC) order remains active beyond the current trading day until it is executed, canceled or reaches an applicable expiration limit.</p>\n<p>The 2014 slides describe a six-month period; actual validity depends on the brokerage's rules and should not be assumed identical today.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The order's price conditions and duration should reflect the investor's requirements. A correctly chosen order type can protect specific trading constraints, but it cannot eliminate uncertainty about execution.</p>\n<p><strong>Examiner's key distinction:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Stop Price}\\neq\\text{Guaranteed Execution Price}}$$</div>\n<p>A stop-loss order attempts to exit after a trigger. A stop-limit order imposes a price boundary but may leave the investor holding the security.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 5 - Transaction costs and investor protection</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Trading profit and net return</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investor purchases:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$500\\text{ shares}\\times \\text{RM}10$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Purchase Cost}=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The purchase commission is RM30.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the total initial cash outflow is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000+30$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}5{,}030}$$</div>\n<p>The investor later sells the shares at RM10.60.</p>\n<p>Gross sale proceeds:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$500(10.60)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}5{,}300$$</div>\n<p>The gross trading profit before commissions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}300-5{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}300}$$</div>\n<p>Total commissions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$30+30=\\text{RM}60$$</div>\n<p>Net trading profit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$300-60$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}240}$$</div>\n<p>Alternatively:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net Profit} = (5{,}300-30)-(5{,}000+30)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=5{,}270-5{,}030$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}240$$</div>\n<p>Since the question asks for the return on the total initial cash outflow:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{240}{5{,}030}\\times100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{net}}\\approx4.77\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Gross profit</th>\n<th>Total commissions</th>\n<th>Net profit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM300</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td><strong>RM240</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor earns a gross price return of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{300}{5{,}000}\\times100=6\\%$$</div>\n<p>but the actual return after the stated transaction costs is lower.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates why investment performance should be evaluated after relevant costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Transaction costs and frequent trading</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter discusses commissions as an important cost of securities transactions.</p>\n<p>Frequent trading increases the number of transactions and therefore may increase total commissions.</p>\n<p>For example, assuming the same RM30 commission applies each time, five round-trip trades would incur:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5(30+30)=\\text{RM}300$$</div>\n<p>in commissions alone.</p>\n<p>If gross trading gains are small, transaction costs can eliminate much of the expected profit.</p>\n<p>The chapter also identifies day trading as risky, particularly when margin is involved, and notes that frequent trading can generate substantial brokerage commissions.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: The investor should not evaluate a trading strategy only by its gross gains. The relevant outcome is the return after trading costs and other applicable expenses.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) SIPC protection and limitations</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) as providing protection against certain financial failures of brokerage firms.</p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish the failure of a brokerage intermediary from losses arising from the investment itself.</p>\n<p>For example, a broker may become insolvent while holding customers' securities or cash.</p>\n<p>SIPC protection concerns certain customer claims associated with such broker failures.</p>\n<p>The 2014 slides state historical protection limits of <span class=\"cur\">$</span>500,000 for securities and <span class=\"cur\">$</span>250,000 for cash. These are the figures printed in the chapter, not a verified statement of current coverage.</p>\n<p>However, the chapter explicitly notes that SIPC does not guarantee investors against churning or poor brokerage advice.</p>\n<p>SIPC protection is also not insurance against ordinary market losses.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An investor whose properly held shares fall in value because of poor company earnings has suffered an investment loss.</li>\n<li>An investor whose broker fails and cannot return protected customer property faces a different kind of loss associated with intermediary failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These situations should not be treated as economically equivalent.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investor protection mechanisms can address certain institutional risks, but they do not remove the possibility of investment losses or guarantee the quality of financial advice.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Mediation versus arbitration</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Mediation</th>\n<th>Arbitration</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nature</td>\n<td>Informal, voluntary dispute-resolution process</td>\n<td>More formal procedure before a panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Method</td>\n<td>Parties attempt to reach an agreement</td>\n<td>Parties present arguments to decision-makers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outcome</td>\n<td>Nonbinding if no agreement is reached</td>\n<td>Can result in a binding decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principal function</td>\n<td>Facilitate a negotiated settlement</td>\n<td>Obtain a formal resolution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Under the chapter's description, binding arbitration requires acceptance of the panel's decision and may involve giving up the right to sue over the dispute.</p>\n<p>Mediation may be preferable when both parties are willing to negotiate a resolution.</p>\n<p>Arbitration may be appropriate when a formal determination is required under an applicable agreement.</p>\n<p>The suitability of either mechanism depends on the nature of the dispute and applicable contractual and regulatory conditions.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investor protection involves different mechanisms for different problems. Brokerage failure, unsuitable advice, excessive trading and ordinary investment losses should not be treated as one undifferentiated category.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 6 - Investment advisors and investment clubs</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Investment advisors and selection criteria</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>An investment advisor provides professional assistance in making investment decisions.</p>\n<p>Depending on the arrangement, an advisor may help clients understand investment choices, analyse securities, develop strategies and manage portfolios.</p>\n<p>The chapter discusses several considerations when choosing an investment advisor.</p>\n<p>First, investors should consider the advisor's experience and track record.</p>\n<p>Second, they should evaluate the advisor's professional qualifications.</p>\n<p>The slides list qualifications such as CFA, CIMA, CFP, CIC, ChFC, CLU and CPA.</p>\n<p>Third, investors should consider the advisor's reputation, fees and ability to understand the client's objectives.</p>\n<p>The chapter emphasizes that a good advisor should ask investors numerous questions to understand their financial expertise and circumstances.</p>\n<p>It also states that regulatory registration does not guarantee competence.</p>\n<p>This means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Registration}\\neq\\text{Guaranteed Competence}}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Professional Advice}\\neq\\text{Guaranteed Returns}}$$</div>\n<p>An investor should therefore evaluate whether the advisor's services offer sufficient benefits relative to their costs and limitations.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Annual advisory fee and net return</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The group has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets Under Management}=\\text{RM}200{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The annual advisory fee is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1.5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Assuming fees are based on beginning-of-year assets:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Annual Fee}=200{,}000(0.015)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}3{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The expected gross investment return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Expected gross earnings:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$200{,}000(0.08)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}16{,}000$$</div>\n<p>After deducting the advisory fee:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net Earnings}=16{,}000-3{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}13{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{13{,}000}{200{,}000}\\times100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{6.5\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Annual return</th>\n<th>Earnings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gross</td>\n<td>8.0%</td>\n<td>RM16,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After advisory fee</td>\n<td><strong>6.5%</strong></td>\n<td>RM13,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Evaluation: The advisor's annual fee reduces the expected return by 1.5 percentage points under the question's assumptions.</p>\n<p>This does not automatically mean that professional advice is poor value.</p>\n<p>The advisor may provide financial planning, risk management, investment research or better decision-making discipline.</p>\n<p>However, those benefits should justify the costs.</p>\n<p>The group should not assume that hiring an advisor guarantees returns exceeding those available through a lower-cost approach.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investment clubs</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines an investment club as a legal partnership established by investors to pool capital and knowledge.</p>\n<p>Members jointly research investment opportunities, recommend securities and analyse investment performance.</p>\n<p>Potential advantages include shared investment knowledge and learning opportunities.</p>\n<p>Pooling financial resources may also allow investors to access opportunities that would be difficult to pursue individually.</p>\n<p>Members can contribute different skills and perspectives to investment analysis.</p>\n<p>However, collective decision-making does not automatically guarantee better decisions.</p>\n<p>Members may disagree about suitable investments, accept excessive risk because of group enthusiasm or fail to examine recommendations critically.</p>\n<p>Therefore, an investment club requires a disciplined decision-making process and clear agreement on investment objectives and responsibilities.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Recommendation</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The group should choose its arrangement based on members' expertise, willingness to manage investments, required services and acceptable costs.</p>\n<p>If the members possess adequate financial knowledge and can independently research securities, discount brokerage services may provide a lower-cost execution mechanism.</p>\n<p>If the group needs substantial professional assistance, employing an investment advisor may be appropriate, provided the expected benefits justify the fees.</p>\n<p>If the members want to combine their capital while developing investment knowledge collaboratively, an investment club may be suitable.</p>\n<p>However, the group should establish clear investment objectives, roles, risk limits and rules for making decisions before pooling funds.</p>\n<p>A particularly sensible approach may involve combining shared investment research with professional advice where specialized expertise is needed.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The most appropriate arrangement is not necessarily the lowest-cost option or the one employing the most professionals. It is the arrangement that provides a suitable balance of expertise, responsibility, services, costs and investment objectives.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Additional short-answer questions for revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These questions are suitable for shorter subjective responses worth approximately 5-10 marks. Try answering each before revealing the model response.</p>\n<p><strong>Q1. Explain the difference between descriptive and analytical investment information. Why is this distinction important?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Descriptive information consists of factual data about historical economic, market, industry, company or investment performance. Analytical information uses current data together with forecasts and recommendations. The distinction matters because observed historical facts do not guarantee the validity of future projections. Investors should verify the data and evaluate the assumptions supporting an investment recommendation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q2. Explain the purpose of Regulation FD, shareholder reports and Form 10-K filings in investment research.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chapter describes Regulation FD as requiring important corporate information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public. Shareholder reports provide companies' annual disclosures to shareholders, while Form 10-K contains annual information filed with the U.S. SEC by publicly traded companies. These sources help investors evaluate companies using more reliable disclosures, although the information still requires analysis and interpretation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why can two stock market indexes produce different returns during the same period?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Indexes can differ in the securities included, the industries represented and the weighting methodology applied. For example, the DJIA covers 30 selected major stocks, while the S&amp;P 500 represents a broader set of 500 companies in the chapter. The Value Line Composite uses equal weighting to reduce the influence of large companies. Consequently, index returns need not be identical even when they are affected by the same general market conditions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q4. Differentiate an odd-lot order and a round-lot order.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Under the textbook's convention, an odd-lot order involves fewer than 100 shares, whereas a round-lot order involves 100 shares or a multiple of 100. These terms describe the quantity of shares ordered, not whether the transaction is a market or limit order. Lot conventions can differ among markets and over time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why should an investor verify the ticker symbol and order details before submitting an online trade?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chapter emphasizes checking ticker symbols, understanding order placement, reviewing order details and confirming completed transactions. A mistaken ticker, quantity, price or order instruction may result in an unintended trade and financial loss. The convenience of online execution makes accurate order entry particularly important because investors may not have a live broker checking their decisions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why does a buy stop order make sense for a short seller?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A short seller profits when the price of the borrowed security declines and suffers a loss when it rises. A buy stop placed above the current market price can activate a purchase order when the price reaches the trigger, helping the investor attempt to close the short position. However, once activated as a market order under the chapter's description, the execution price is not guaranteed.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q7. Why can excessive day trading reduce an investor's net return even when many trades are profitable?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Every transaction may incur brokerage commissions and other trading costs. Frequent trading increases those expenses. A strategy generating many small gross profits can therefore produce little or no net profit once costs are deducted. The chapter also warns that day trading is especially risky when margin borrowing is involved.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q8. Evaluate the statement that an investment advisor with professional qualifications is necessarily superior to an investment club.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The statement is unjustified without examining investor objectives, expertise, services and costs. A qualified advisor may provide professional research and guidance, but registration and qualifications do not guarantee competence or investment returns. Investment clubs can facilitate pooled research and investor education, although collective decisions can still be poor. The superior arrangement depends on whether the expected benefits justify the costs and responsibilities involved.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. How to allocate your marks and time</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The depth of your answer should follow the question's mark allocation.</p>\n<p>A reasonable practice framework for a 20-mark analytical question is:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Illustrative marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Accurate definitions and terminology</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explanation of financial mechanisms</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application to the scenario and calculations</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation, limitations and justified conclusion</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is not your lecturer's actual marking scheme. Individual questions may allocate marks very differently, especially those involving calculations.</p>\n<p>When asked to compare, establish meaningful differences rather than writing unrelated definitions.</p>\n<p>When asked to analyse, explain cause and effect. For example, explain why a limit order can remain unfilled even when the investor wants to buy.</p>\n<p>When asked to evaluate, identify the trade-off, examine limitations and justify which alternative is more appropriate.</p>\n<p>When asked to recommend, make a specific choice consistent with the scenario. Merely listing several possible order types is not a recommendation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. What I would prioritize for Chapter 3</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Highest priority - Securities orders</p>\n<p>Master market, limit, stop-loss and stop-limit orders. Practise applying them to rapid price movements and explaining execution certainty versus price protection.</p>\n<p>High priority - Investment information and online research</p>\n<p>Distinguish descriptive from analytical information, identify reliable information sources, and evaluate misleading recommendations and online investment risks.</p>\n<p>High priority - Market indexes and benchmarking</p>\n<p>Explain how market averages and indexes work, compare major indicators and evaluate whether a benchmark appropriately represents the portfolio.</p>\n<p>High priority - Brokerage firms and accounts</p>\n<p>Compare brokerage services, cash and margin accounts, and explain why investor expertise, advisory costs and incentives affect broker selection.</p>\n<p>Important - Transaction costs and protection</p>\n<p>Calculate net trading returns and distinguish ordinary market losses from the brokerage failures addressed by SIPC. Understand mediation and arbitration.</p>\n<p>Important - Investment advisors and clubs</p>\n<p>Evaluate professional advice, compensation, competence and collective investment research rather than assuming any arrangement automatically produces superior performance.</p>\n<p>These priorities reflect how readily each topic supports technical and higher-order examination questions, not confirmed exam weightings.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Final high-level examination exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Consider this statement:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"An investor who obtains the most accurate information and identifies the best-performing stock will necessarily achieve a superior investment return, regardless of the trading order, brokerage firm or transaction costs.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A high-scoring answer should challenge the reasoning at several levels.</p>\n<p>Reliable information can improve the quality of investment analysis, but even accurate historical data cannot guarantee future share-price performance.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the investor's realized outcome is affected by how the trade is executed. A market order may fill rapidly but at an unfavorable price, while a limit order protects against unacceptable execution prices but may never be filled.</p>\n<p>Transaction costs can reduce net returns, particularly when an investor trades frequently. Broker selection can also affect the costs, services and advice available to the investor.</p>\n<p>Finally, market performance must be evaluated using an appropriate benchmark. A positive return may still constitute relative underperformance.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Investment Outcome} = f(\\text{Information, Analysis, Execution, Costs, Risk}) }$$</div>\n<p>The central lesson of Chapter 3 is that selecting an attractive investment is only one part of a successful investment process. The investor must also obtain reliable information, interpret it correctly, choose appropriate intermediaries, execute transactions suitably and evaluate the net results.</p>\n<p>That is the level of integrated reasoning you should aim to demonstrate in your subjective examination.</p>\n<p>Source note: Prepared using your uploaded Chapter 3 - Investment Information and Securities Transactions (Pearson, 2014). The historical U.S. market references, brokerage fee illustrations, SEC/SIPC descriptions and order-duration conventions are used for textbook exam preparation, not as verified statements of current Malaysian or U.S. requirements. All mock cases and marking allocations are original practice material.</p></div></details></div></details>");
